Done. Committed 46cc56c ("Part of #1184"). Build, the full cargo test run, clippy with -D warnings, fmt --check and tests/harness_logic.sh (336/0) all pass.

What changed for users: a long list written over several lines, like

```toml
[permissions]
deny = [
  "rm *",
]
```

used to set no deny rule at all, and the startup warning said "This rule is NOT in force". It now works, and so does the same list under [directories]. Inline tables (permissions = { deny = [...] }) work too. Before, they got two warnings and set no rules either.

I checked this with the built binary on the assessment's temp repo: yoyo doctor no longer prints that warning, and yoyo permissions lists ✗ rm * and ✗ /etc.

How:
• parse_permissions_from_config and parse_directories_from_config now read allow and deny from the toml crate's parsed table (new config_toml_read::fence_list). Their signatures are unchanged, so no caller had to change.
• #1184 metric: I ran grep -n "content.lines()\|parse_toml_array\|\.lines()" src/config.rs src/config_value.rs src/config_unknown_keys.rs | wc -l. It found 21 before, 14 after.
• Old warnings removed: the line-by-line warning in config_value.rs now skips both tables. In config_unknown_keys.rs, inline fence tables are no longer reported as "not read"; only a key other than allow/deny inside one is flagged. The comparison against Python's TOML parser (tomllib) now gives 56 of 56 shapes agreeing, with 0 refused (it was 7).
• Wrong types still warn loudly. A value that isn't a list (deny = "rm *"), or entries that aren't strings ([1, "rm *"]), print could not read all of '[permissions] deny': … Those rules are NOT in force. The string entries in a mixed list still apply, so one bad entry doesn't drop the whole deny list. Before, a plain string was ignored with no warning, and a bare 1 became the rule "1" (I got that from reading the old code, not by running it).

Two files outside the planned list:
• `config_unknown_keys.rs`: without this edit, the inline-table warning would still have said "not in force" about rules that now apply.
• `cd_config_note.rs`: /cd reads the new directory's .yoyo.toml without the startup check that refuses invalid TOML. With the new reader, an invalid file would quietly produce an empty deny list. It now counts as Unreadable, whose existing note says none of the file is applied. This has its own test.

Tests: one row per spelling in config_toml_tests.rs, for both tables and both keys, each compared as the whole list:
• header with a one-line list
• list over several lines
• comments inside and after the list
• the #1177 comment after the value
• dotted keys
• inline table
• [ T ] with spaces
• single-quoted strings with a trailing comma

There is also a near-miss: an unknown key under [permissions] sets no rule and still gets the unknown-key warning. Six older tests pinned the old behaviour; I rewrote them to test what is true now and renamed the ones whose names had become false.

Recall control: I broke both parsers (2 NEUTERED markers, counted before the run). The spelling test failed by name on [permissions] header, array over several lines. I restored them in the same command and it passed again. I posted that result and the before/after numbers on #1184 before running the full test suite.

Also updated: docs/src/configuration/permissions.md said inline tables are not read. It now describes every accepted spelling, with a multi-line example. In tests/module_size.rs, config.rs went from 4739 to 4700 lines and I lowered its entry to match.

Still open on #1184:
• Writes to the config file (set_toml_key). From reading the code, not running it: when yoyo adds an allow pattern to a list that spans several lines, it replaces only the first line, which would leave the file broken.
• parse_toml_array still has two production callers, cli.rs:2300 and cd_config_note.rs:100, both for the top-level mcp = [...] value.
• The line-by-line scanners in config_value::unreadable_values and config_unknown_keys::unknown_config_keys.
• The tomllib comparison test.

